WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.600 --> 00:00:05.789
The math inside stretto, as the code computes it.

2
00:00:06.289 --> 00:00:10.891
One event to estimate, one rule to act on it, and how well both held up.

3
00:00:12.691 --> 00:00:16.707
A lookup pays off if the agent uses it before its next write.

4
00:00:17.157 --> 00:00:19.817
So the quantity to estimate is q:

5
00:00:19.817 --> 00:00:23.744
the chance a read is among those the agent makes before it writes.

6
00:00:24.244 --> 00:00:27.664
It is never smaller than the chance the read comes next,

7
00:00:27.664 --> 00:00:29.768
because a reply can come between.

8
00:00:30.268 --> 00:00:36.188
After a user's details, the habit put an order next at 0.64.

9
00:00:36.188 --> 00:00:40.238
The agents read one before the write 94% of the time.

10
00:00:40.688 --> 00:00:47.822
After an order, a product came next at 0.08, but before the write 38% of the time.

11
00:00:49.622 --> 00:00:53.607
A flow makes only reads, and reads leave the state as it was.

12
00:00:54.057 --> 00:00:58.430
If the agent makes the same calls, every write lands on the same state.

13
00:00:58.880 --> 00:01:02.480
A wrong lookup changes only what the agent reads.

14
00:01:02.480 --> 00:01:04.125
Its worst case is a detour.

15
00:01:05.825 --> 00:01:09.145
Used, a lookup saves a turn, worth beta.

16
00:01:09.145 --> 00:01:11.875
Unused, it is a detour, which costs delta.

17
00:01:12.325 --> 00:01:16.125
Its expected value is the saving, q times beta,

18
00:01:16.125 --> 00:01:19.892
less the expected detour, one minus q, times delta.

19
00:01:20.392 --> 00:01:24.592
As q rises, the saving grows and the detour shrinks.

20
00:01:24.592 --> 00:01:27.344
They balance at delta over beta plus delta.

21
00:01:27.844 --> 00:01:35.924
Live on τ²-bench, 260 saved turns saved 1.56 million input tokens,

22
00:01:35.924 --> 00:01:39.104
and 17 detours carried 43,000.

23
00:01:39.554 --> 00:01:47.691
That is about 6,000 a turn and 2,530 a detour, so the line sits at 0.30.

24
00:01:48.141 --> 00:01:55.785
Counted in airline and telecom, where detours are shorter, it sits at 0.12 to 0.13.

25
00:01:57.685 --> 00:01:59.985
The chances come from counts.

26
00:01:59.985 --> 00:02:04.515
Each step becomes its tool, its outcome, and a feature of its result.

27
00:02:04.965 --> 00:02:07.605
The habit predicts the next step from the last two,

28
00:02:07.605 --> 00:02:10.252
and backs off to shorter contexts.

29
00:02:10.702 --> 00:02:15.242
Each context is a Dirichlet–multinomial: its counts,

30
00:02:15.242 --> 00:02:18.315
plus alpha times what the shorter context predicts.

31
00:02:18.765 --> 00:02:20.545
In the quickstart's sessions,

32
00:02:20.545 --> 00:02:24.653
each longer context sharpens the guess that an order comes next.

33
00:02:25.103 --> 00:02:27.143
Turn alpha up, and the prior takes over.

34
00:02:27.143 --> 00:02:29.597
Turn it down, and the counts do.

35
00:02:30.897 --> 00:02:35.537
The probability of use counts the same contexts for a different event:

36
00:02:35.537 --> 00:02:37.762
each read made before the next write.

37
00:02:38.212 --> 00:02:42.486
Each tool is its own coin, so these chances need not sum to one.

38
00:02:44.286 --> 00:02:47.666
Alpha sets how fast the counts outweigh the prior.

39
00:02:47.666 --> 00:02:49.399
stretto doesn't tune it by hand.

40
00:02:49.849 --> 00:02:54.471
It infers it with fugue, a probabilistic programming library for Rust.

41
00:02:54.921 --> 00:02:57.981
The program draws log alpha from a normal prior,

42
00:02:57.981 --> 00:03:01.193
and scores the sessions one step at a time.

43
00:03:01.643 --> 00:03:04.339
Prior times likelihood is the posterior.

44
00:03:04.789 --> 00:03:09.114
Metropolis–Hastings walks it, and the draws pile up into its shape.

45
00:03:09.564 --> 00:03:14.244
On the quickstart's six sessions, the median is 0.70:

46
00:03:14.244 --> 00:03:16.333
the alpha the flow was learned with.

47
00:03:18.333 --> 00:03:21.173
A lookup also needs its arguments.

48
00:03:21.173 --> 00:03:23.190
stretto counts where each one came from.

49
00:03:23.640 --> 00:03:26.380
A binding's chance is right over tried,

50
00:03:26.380 --> 00:03:30.768
with one added to the right and two to the tried: a uniform prior.

51
00:03:31.218 --> 00:03:36.653
Each binding tried narrows it: 12 right of 14 gives 0.81.

52
00:03:37.103 --> 00:03:40.483
The decision multiplies the two: the read's chance,

53
00:03:40.483 --> 00:03:42.977
times its arguments', against the line.

54
00:03:44.777 --> 00:03:50.576
A chance is calibrated if lookups scored 0.7 are used seven times in ten.

55
00:03:51.026 --> 00:03:55.266
Scored on the next step, retail's lookups sit above the line.

56
00:03:55.266 --> 00:03:57.023
The next step understates use.

57
00:03:57.473 --> 00:04:01.163
Scored on use before the next write, they sit closer to it.

58
00:04:01.613 --> 00:04:04.613
The calibration error fell in every domain.

59
00:04:04.613 --> 00:04:10.073
In retail, from 0.164 to 0.083.

60
00:04:10.073 --> 00:04:10.879
Lower is better.

61
00:04:12.779 --> 00:04:15.759
Better chances make better decisions.

62
00:04:15.759 --> 00:04:20.311
Here is each threshold's net saving, for GLM-5 in retail.

63
00:04:20.761 --> 00:04:24.161
Deciding on use before the write peaks at the counted line,

64
00:04:24.161 --> 00:04:26.031
and falls slowly past it.

65
00:04:26.481 --> 00:04:31.344
The next-step rule falls away past one half, where its chances seldom reach.

66
00:04:31.794 --> 00:04:38.134
Over nine agents it never saw, it takes 86.4% of retail's ceiling:

67
00:04:38.134 --> 00:04:41.074
10.2 points more than the next-step rule.

68
00:04:42.974 --> 00:04:53.112
Live, with no model of its own, it cut GLM-5.3's LLM turns by 27.9% over 28 tasks.

69
00:04:53.562 --> 00:04:56.402
Each line is one task, before and after.

70
00:04:56.402 --> 00:04:59.514
23 of the 28 took fewer turns.

71
00:04:59.964 --> 00:05:02.964
And in replay, it learns fast:

72
00:05:02.964 --> 00:05:08.232
ten of an agent's own sessions give 96% of what all of them do in retail.

73
00:05:10.132 --> 00:05:12.556
A flow is audited as a program too.

74
00:05:12.956 --> 00:05:16.836
Each decision is a categorical choice at its own address:

75
00:05:16.836 --> 00:05:19.483
a lookup, handing back, or another step.

76
00:05:19.933 --> 00:05:25.250
fugue scores the agent's real path under it, in nats of surprise per decision.

77
00:05:27.450 --> 00:05:31.390
The paper gives the proofs and every benchmark number.

78
00:05:31.390 --> 00:05:34.677
The worked examples are the walkthrough's and the quickstart's.
